financial markets in russia had been  volatile for weeks leading up to the  country's invasion of ukraine and we saw  a sell-off after the u.s and numerous  other industrialized nations imposed  sanctions on russia when it moved into  the sovereign nation one of the most  significant results of all that has been  the historic loss of value for russia's  currency the ruble i'll explain why that  is how it works and what it could mean  for russia and its conflict with ukraine  going forward first let me explain why  the value of russia's currency is so  important currencies are traded on  markets just like stocks or other  investments where investors decide every  day what a country's dollar or peso or  ruble is worth when a country's economy  improves typically its interest rates  rise and it attracts outside money so  the value of its currency increases when  the economy slows down the central bank  is usually cutting interest rates in an  effort to improve things and the value  of the currency typically falls a weak  currency makes it expensive to buy  imports because many are priced in  dollars so even if the price of an item  doesn't change when a currency loses  value it costs more for that country's  citizens to buy that also raises  inflation and creates problems for  businesses russia's currency has been  losing value since 2014 when russian  president vladimir putin decided to  invade crimea and face sanctions from  the united states european union and  others  one u.s dollar bought around 32 russian  rubles at the beginning of 2014. by the  end of 2019 it was about 62 rubles at  the start of 2022 one dollar was worth  around 73 rupees then came the invasion  of ukraine  sanctions from the u.s europe and others  and the removal of russia's access to  the swift global payment system that is  big my colleague shelby holliday has a  great video about swift what it is and  why it matters i've left a link to that  video in the comments check it out if  you want to know more economic sanctions  like the ones imposed on russia are  orders by countries that ban their  companies or financial institutions from  doing business with firms based in the  sanctioned country the sanctions imposed  so far largely stopped the flow of  european and american money into much of  russia's financial system that can choke  off a country's economy because its  businesses simply can't do business with  large portions of the world's financial  system the ruble fell to a record low  value of 85 to 1 versus the dollar after  the invasion and to more than 100 rubles  per dollar in the following days in  addition to boosting inflation a weak  currency also causes capital flight  investors pull their money out of the  local currency and put it in one scene  as more stable typically dollars or  euros in response russia has imposed  capital controls and its central bank  has raised the country's interest rates  to more than 20 percent this could be  the start of a vicious circle high  interest rates means it's more expensive  to borrow money which slows down the  economy and rising inflation makes goods  and services more expensive and harder  to obtain this is the goal of sanctions  hurt a country's economy so much that  its leaders decide to change their  actions in this case that means pulling  back on the invasion of ukraine however  russia still has a massive stockpile of  dollars euros and gold and it also  provides about 12 percent of the world's  oil so it's unlikely that russia will  fold overnight outside of russia this  turmoil hasn't undermined investors  confidence while stock prices have been  hurt by the conflict u.s and european  markets haven't seen anything like the  sell-off in russian assets currencies  tell an important story an investor  should watch the ruble if they want to  see how much better or worse things are  getting in russia if you enjoyed this  video hit the like button and make sure  to subscribe to keep up with my latest  videos on markets and finance i'll see  you next time
 

